  "query_preview": "..\nToday and Saturday: Widespread wildfire smoke remains in place\nacross the region this afternoon. Some modest improvement has\nbeen noted on satellite and surface observations to the \nsouthwest across the lower Columbia Basin, northern Oregon, and \nthrough the Kittitas Valley where strenghening westerly winds \nare pushing smoke to the east. Smoke output fields from the RRFS\nand HRRR high-resolution weather models suggest modest \nimprovement in smoke across the Columbia Basin and northeast \nWashington into northwest Idaho this evening, with more notable \nimprovement over the weekend as westerly winds continue to \ntransport smoke to the east. That said, ongoing regional \nwildfires will almost certainly continue to produce smoke which \nwill keep at least some smoke in place across the Pacific \nNorthwest through the weekend; smoke will be thickest in the \nvicinity of ongoing wildfires and within smoke plumes that \nextend downwind of the fires.\n\nA couple of shortwaves passing through a flattening upper-level\nridge of high pressure will facilitate breezy conditions today\nand Saturday. At the surface, a dry air mass is in place, and\nhot temperatures have driven relative humidity below critical\nthresholds. Red Flag Warnings remain in effect today and \nSaturday for much of the region including the Cascade gaps and \nColumbia Basin due to the aforementioned breezy westerly winds \nand low relative humidity. Strongest winds are expected through \nthe Cascade gaps and across the Waterville Plateau.\n\nSunday through Tuesday: A quasi-zonal 500-hPa pattern is\nadvertised by ensemble NWP guidance through the period, with a\nnotable shortwave passage on Sunday. Diurnally breezy westerly \nwinds will continue through the Cascade gaps and into the \nColumbia Basin each day. Coupled with low afternoon relative \nhumidity, elevated fire weather conditions will continue to be \npresent each day. Temperatures are forecast to return to within \nseveral degrees of normal, generally in the mid-80s to lower 90s\nduring the afternoon for larger population centers.\n\nWednesday through Friday: Ensemble NWP guidance continues to\nadvertise some potential for a brief pattern change. Clusters of\nlike-solutioned ensemble members indicate roughly a 50% chance\nof a trough across southern BC or northern Washington Wednesday\nand Thursday. This solution would bring precipitation chances to\nthe region, mainly over the mountains, and westerly followed by\nnortherly winds at the surface. Strongest winds would be through\nthe Cascade gaps and Okanogan Valley.\n\n-CTP",
